---
import DocPage from '../components/DocPage.astro';
import { commitments, legal, site, url } from '../config';

const cards = commitments;

/** Only published when the client confirms the data in writing. */
const facts = [
  { label: 'Legal name', value: legal.entity },
  { label: 'Entity', value: legal.entityType },
  {
    label: 'License',
    value: legal.license
      ? `${legal.license}${legal.licenseAuthority ? ` — ${legal.licenseAuthority}` : ''}`
      : 'Provided in writing with every proposal and contract',
  },
  {
    label: 'Insurance',
    value: legal.insurance || 'Certificate of insurance provided with every proposal',
  },
  { label: 'Service area', value: 'Miami-Dade and Broward Counties, Florida' },
  { label: 'Address', value: `${site.street}, ${site.city}` },
];
---

<DocPage
  title={`Licensing, permits and consumer rights — ${site.name}`}
  description="How Adima Design LLC works in Florida: contractor licensing, permits, written contracts, the 3-day right to cancel, lien waivers and warranty terms for venetian plaster, microcement and epoxy work in Miami and Fort Lauderdale."
  eyebrow="Legal & consumer protection"
  heading="Who you are hiring, and what protects you."
  lead="Decorative finishes are construction work. In Florida that comes with rules that exist for your benefit — licensing, written contracts, permits, lien notices and a cancellation window. Here is exactly how we work inside them."
  active="legal"
>
  <h2 id="who">1. The company you are hiring</h2>
  <dl class="doc-facts">
    {facts.map((f) => (
      <div>
        <dt>{f.label}</dt>
        <dd>{f.value}</dd>
      </div>
    ))}
  </dl>
  <p>
    Adima Design LLC is a Florida limited liability company. We design and apply interior finishes —
    venetian plaster, marmorino, microcement, limewash and epoxy resin — for homes, offices and
    commercial spaces in South Florida. We do not perform electrical, plumbing, structural,
    roofing or HVAC work, and we do not subcontract those trades without telling you in writing.
  </p>
  {legal.license ? (
    <p>
      You can verify our license yourself in the State of Florida database:{' '}
      <a href={legal.licenseLookup} target="_blank" rel="noopener">DBPR license lookup</a>. Local
      jurisdictions (Miami-Dade County, City of Miami, Broward County) may also require a
      certificate of competency — ask us for the one that applies to your address.
    </p>
  ) : (
    <p>
      Florida requires a licensed or registered contractor for this type of work, and the license
      number must appear on every proposal, bid, contract and advertisement
      (<abbr title="Florida Statutes">F.S.</abbr> 489.119(5)(b)). Our license and certificate of
      insurance are sent with your written proposal — ask for them before you sign anything, and
      look us up in the{' '}
      <a href={legal.licenseLookup} target="_blank" rel="noopener">DBPR license lookup</a>.
    </p>
  )}

  <h2 id="commitments">2. What we put in writing</h2>
  <ul class="doc-cards">
    {cards.map((c) => (
      <li>
        <strong>{c.title}</strong>
        <span>{c.text}</span>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h2 id="contract">3. Your contract, deposit and schedule</h2>
  <p>
    Every job starts with a written proposal that states scope, materials and products, number of
    coats, sheen, square footage, price, payment milestones and start date. Nothing is charged
    before you approve it.
  </p>
  <p>
    If a residential contract requires an initial payment above 10% of the contract price, Florida
    law (F.S. 489.126) requires the contractor to apply for the necessary permits within 30 days
    and to start work within 90 days after the permits are issued, unless you agree in writing to
    a longer period. Using your deposit for another job is a crime in Florida, not just a breach of
    contract.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cancel">4. Your 3-business-day right to cancel</h2>
  <p>
    If you sign at your home (a home solicitation sale), you may cancel until midnight of the third
    business day after signing — no reason needed (F.S. 501.025). Tell us in writing, by hand, mail
    or message. If work has not started, you get your deposit back. We put this notice on the
    agreement itself so it is never a surprise.
  </p>

  <h2 id="permits">5. Permits and inspections</h2>
  <p>
    Most interior decorative finishes over a sound substrate do not need a building permit. Work
    that changes the structure, adds moisture-sensitive assemblies, touches exterior stucco
    cladding, or involves electrical or plumbing does. We tell you which case you are in during the
    estimate, and where a permit applies we pull it under our license and coordinate the
    inspection.
  </p>

  <h2 id="liens">6. Liens: what the notices mean</h2>
  <p>
    Florida's Construction Lien Law (Chapter 713) protects subcontractors and suppliers, which is
    why you may receive a <em>Notice to Owner</em> even though you already paid us: it must be
    served within 45 days of first furnishing labor or materials (F.S. 713.06), and a lien must be
    recorded within 90 days of the last work (F.S. 713.08). Receiving one is not a demand for
    money — it is a warning that someone down the chain wants to preserve a claim.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>We give you a list of the subs and suppliers we use, on request.</li>
    <li>Every payment is exchanged for a lien release covering the work paid for.</li>
    <li>
      Before your final payment we provide the contractor's final payment affidavit
      (F.S. 713.06(3)(d)) so you can pay the rest without risk.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="warranty">7. Warranty and care</h2>
  <p>
    {legal.warranty
      ? `Workmanship is covered by our ${legal.warranty}.`
      : 'Workmanship warranty terms are stated in your contract.'}{' '}
    Products carry their manufacturer's warranty, and we hand over the technical data and care
    sheet for every product we use. Excluded: damage from moisture intrusion or leaks, structural
    movement or cracking of the substrate, impact, abrasion, harsh chemicals, and cleaning with
    anything other than the methods on the care sheet. Touch-ups and repairs after the warranty
    period are quoted separately.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reviews">8. Reviews and testimonials</h2>
  <p>
    Every testimonial on this site is written by a real client, published as written, never bought
    and never edited to change its meaning. That is not only our policy — since October 2024 the
    FTC's rule on consumer reviews (16 C.F.R. Part 465) makes fake reviews, purchased reviews and
    review suppression illegal nationwide. You can ask us to remove your testimonial at any time
    and we will.
  </p>

  <h2 id="complaints">9. If something goes wrong</h2>
  <p>
    Start with us: {site.email} · {site.phone}. We answer in writing and we put a remedy plan with
    dates in the reply. If that does not resolve it, Florida gives you independent routes:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>DBPR / Construction Industry Licensing Board</strong> — complaint against a licensed
      contractor, including unlicensed activity reports.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Florida Attorney General</strong> — deceptive trade practices under the Florida
      Deceptive and Unfair Trade Practices Act (F.S. 501, Part II).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Homeowners' Construction Recovery Fund</strong> — after a final judgment or
      arbitration award against a state-certified contractor, recovery may be available under F.S.
      489.141.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Small claims / county court</strong> — for disputes within the statutory limits.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="law">10. Governing law and intellectual property</h2>
  <p>
    These terms are governed by the laws of the {legal.governingLaw}, without regard to conflict
    rules, and any dispute not otherwise resolved belongs in the courts of {legal.venue}. The
    photographs, text and finish samples on this site are the property of {legal.entity} or the
    client who commissioned the work; they may not be reused commercially without permission.
  </p>

  <h2 id="accessibility">11. Accessibility</h2>
  <p>
    This site targets WCAG 2.1 AA: keyboard navigation, visible focus, readable contrast and no
    motion when your system asks for reduced motion. If any part of it is hard to use, email{' '}
    <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> and we will fix it or send the information in
    another format.
  </p>

  <p class="doc-updated">
    Last updated {legal.updated}. Questions about this page: <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>.
    See also our <a href={url('/privacy')}>Privacy notice</a>.
  </p>

  <p class="doc-note">
    This page explains how we work in plain language; it is not legal advice to you. Statute
    references are to the 2024 Florida Statutes.
  </p>
</DocPage>
